            <ArticleTitle>Organic Nanotheranostics for Photoacoustic Imaging-Guided Phototherapy.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2174/0929867324666170921103152</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Phototherapies including photothermal therapy (PTT) and photodynamic therapy (PDT) have emerged as one of the avant-garde strategies for cancer treatment. Photoacoustic (PA) imaging is a new hybrid imaging modality that shows great promise for real-time in vivo monitoring of biological processes with deep tissue penetration and high spatial resolution. To enhance therapeutic efficiency and reduce side effects, it is necessary to explore the combination of cancer therapy and diagnosis. Nanotheranostics composed of PTT/PDT and PA imaging are emerging to provide unprecedented advantages in accelerating cancer theranostics. This review summarizes the recent progress of using organic nanomaterials including near-infrared (NIR) dyes and semiconducting polymer nanoparticles (SPNs) for PA imaging guided cancer phototherapy, and also addresses the present challenges and potential of PA imaging guided PTT/PDT.</AbstractText>
